The snippet is sufficient because it shows the exact rcu_read_unlock_special() deferral path and the deferred-QS handling introduced by this commit. The vulnerable construct is present in rcu_read_unlock_special():

`if ((preempt_bh_were_disabled || irqs_were_disabled) &&
    t->rcu_read_unlock_special.b.blocked) {
        /* Need to defer quiescent state until everything is enabled. */
        raise_softirq_irqoff(RCU_SOFTIRQ);
        local_irq_restore(flags);
        return;
}`

This raises `RCU_SOFTIRQ` while deferring the quiescent-state work, but it does not first mark the per-CPU deferred work as pending (for example by setting `rdp->deferred_qs = true` before the softirq raise). The later helper `rcu_preempt_deferred_qs_irqrestore()` clearly expects such state to exist, as shown by:

`rdp = this_cpu_ptr(rcu_state_p->rda);`
`if (!special.s && !rdp->deferred_qs) { ... return; }`

and:

`if (special.b.exp_need_qs || rdp->deferred_qs) {
        t->rcu_read_unlock_special.b.exp_need_qs = false;
        rdp->deferred_qs = false;
        rcu_report_exp_rdp(...);
... }
`

So this commit introduces deferred-QS machinery, but in the shown deferral branch it only calls `raise_softirq_irqoff(RCU_SOFTIRQ)` and returns, without setting the deferred flag first. Per the stated root cause, that omission allows `rcu_read_unlock_special()`/deferred handling to be re-entered after softirq raising because recursion protection had been removed elsewhere. Therefore the vulnerable construct is present, and the fix is not yet in this function.
